FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] phases/phase-075/FINDINGS.md:15 — the two out-of-scope repairs are recorded with a new ledger status `[RESOLVED — verify in owner phase]` instead of the protocol-mandated `[OPEN]` (AGENTS.md §"Out-of-Scope Findings & Defect Protocol" says the ledger entry must be `[OPEN]` so it is auto-queued in Section 14). The owner phases 073/088 may therefore never see that a foreign file (`tests/bench/mod.rs`, `phases/phase-088/scan.rs`) was edited on their behalf. — keep the rows `[OPEN]` (or add an explicit owner-verification row) so the queue still reaches the owners.
2. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1359 — `standard_module_member` derives the receiver as `index - 2` and `standard_module_of` (src/lsp.rs:1330-1351) accepts *any* unbound identifier that names a stdlib module. In a chained access such as `record.files.read` where `files` is not a statically-known field/method (so `token_binding[files]` is `None`), `read` is reported as the `files.read` builtin even though the receiver is a member value, not the module. — require the receiver token to be a free name (not itself in `member_names`), or reject a receiver whose preceding token is `.`. (Traced through `is_member_access` → `member_binding` returning `None` → `standard_module_of` falling through to `receiver_name`.)
3. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:5664 — the new standard-module navigation surface tests `find_references` inside a `{…}` interpolation but never `goto_definition` inside one; the `standard_module_member` branch in `definition_at_token` reached through `goto_definition_in_interpolation` is therefore untested for this new kind of target. — add a fixture asserting `goto_definition` on `{files.read}` maps back to the `files.read` builtin span.

